Source: the `prime` gem 0.1.4 (https://github.com/ruby/prime), its own `sig/` directory
Files:  sig/prime.rbs             -> prime.rbs
        sig/integer-extension.rbs -> integer-extension.rbs
        (sig/manifest.yaml is not copied: its one dependency, `singleton`, is
        the library `RbsLoader::LIBRARY_SUPPLEMENT_VENDORED_DIRS` gates this
        directory on.)
SHA-256 of the upstream files as shipped in prime-0.1.4.gem:
        412db65a4774a53eb6e7df9408a0dce40d7907612b8ca3b2a12f2486f62552c3  sig/prime.rbs
        2af60558aa353e4d183557f6091978a915d682fabb9a95a3d87e77dc7ba129f5  sig/integer-extension.rbs
License: Ruby's license or the 2-clause BSD license (gemspec: "Ruby", "BSD-2-Clause");
         this copy is used under the 2-clause BSD license, reproduced below.

Local change (Rigor): `integer-extension.rbs` declares
`Integer.from_prime_division: (Array[[ String ]]) -> Integer` upstream. The
method takes the `[[prime, exponent], ...]` pairs `Integer#prime_division`
returns, so the copy here reads `(Array[[ Integer, Integer ]]) -> Integer`;
verbatim, `Integer.from_prime_division([[2, 2], [3, 1]])` drew a false
`call.argument-type-mismatch`. Nothing else differs from upstream.

Loading (issue #1700): this directory declares a top-level `class Prime` and
reopens `Integer`, so it loads only when the project requires `prime` (or lists
it under `libraries:`) and a trial build finds it clashes with no other
signature source (`Rigor::Environment::RequiredFeatures`, `RbsLoader`).

